  4. Spark Streaming Scenarios You Can Present

  Since you have strong Spark knowledge, here are the scenarios you can explain as downstream processing of the S3 data lake (or if
  consuming directly from Kafka via Spark Structured Streaming):

  Late Events

  Event occurred at 10:00 AM but arrived at 10:45 AM

  Solution: Watermarking
    df.withWatermark("event_time", "1 hour")
      .groupBy(window("event_time", "10 minutes"))

  - Events within watermark threshold → processed into correct window
  - Events beyond threshold → dropped (too late to matter)
  - Trade-off: longer watermark = more state to maintain

  Out-of-Order Events

  Event A (10:01) arrives after Event B (10:05)

  Solution: Watermark + Event-time windowing (not processing-time)
    - Window by event_time, not arrival_time
    - Watermark holds window open long enough for stragglers
    - Spark sorts within window before aggregation

  Duplicate Events

  Same event delivered twice (at-least-once delivery from Kafka)

  Solution: Deduplication
    df.dropDuplicates(["event_id", "event_time"])

    Or idempotent writes:
    - Write with mode("append") + partition by unique key
    - Use Delta Lake MERGE for upsert semantics

    Kafka itself: consumer group + offset tracking prevents reprocessing
    But MSK → Lambda is at-least-once, so Lambda/downstream must handle it

  Spike/Surge Events

  Normal: 100 events/sec → Spike: 10,000 events/sec

  Already handled by architecture:
    - SQS absorbs burst (unlimited queue depth)
    - Lambda concurrency limit prevents downstream overwhelm
    - Firehose auto-scales
    - MSK partitions distribute load across brokers

  Spark side:
    - maxOffsetsPerTrigger limits how much Spark reads per micro-batch
    - Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) handles skewed partitions
    - Back-pressure: Spark reads at its own pace from Kafka offsets



